WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:03.120
INTRO:
We all know Netflix is massive. They were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.360
OG streaming service that destroyed Blockbuster&nbsp;
and gave cable TV providers a run for their money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.400
But, did you know that Netflix was not only a&nbsp;
strong performing stock but the best performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:28.800
stock in the S&amp;P 500 for an entire decade? If you&nbsp;
invested money in Netflix at the start of 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.240
you would’ve nearly 100xed your money at the&nbsp;
peak. For perspective, with Amazon, you would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.280
roughly 33xed your money, and with Apple, you&nbsp;
would’ve roughly 25xed your money. Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.000
this stellar performance, Netflix is often&nbsp;
grouped with the top tech companies in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.760
even though it’s only a fraction of the size. Once&nbsp;
the pandemic hit, Netflix stock just skyrocketed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.280
even more as people binged shows and movies on&nbsp;
Netflix as they didn’t know what else to do. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.520
over the past couple of months, Netflix hasn’t&nbsp;
been doing so hot. In fact, Netflix is down over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:08.880
50% within just the last 4 months which correlates&nbsp;
to a $150 billion loss in market cap. This makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.200
Netflix the 5th worst performer in the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;
So, here’s how Netflix went from being the #1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.720
stock in the S&amp;P 500 for a decade to being part&nbsp;
of the bottom 1% all within a matter of months.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.760
DEPENDENCY ISSUES:&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.720
One of Netflix’s most deep-rooted problems&nbsp;
is that they’re dependent on traditional TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.520
and movie industries for content. In other&nbsp;
words, Netflix’s ability to have a large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.080
diverse library of content is extremely dependent&nbsp;
on how many licensing deals they’re able to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.280
with production studios. Now, Netflix has been&nbsp;
well aware of this since the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.760
and that’s why one of their biggest focuses has&nbsp;
been producing Netflix originals. And over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.000
past decade, they’ve pumped out several&nbsp;
phenomenal series like BoJack Horseman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.640
House of Cards, and of course Squid Game.&nbsp;
Aside from these originals, they’ve also bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.680
tremendous shows like Money Heist and Lucifer.&nbsp;
But at the end of the day, Netflix’s original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.960
content and strategic purchases only account for a&nbsp;
fraction of their entire library. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.680
if production studios were to boycott Netflix for&nbsp;
one reason or another, Netflix would lose most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.480
its content and probably a significant portion&nbsp;
of their subscribers. From the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.520
many studios have refused to license their content&nbsp;
to Netflix, and with time, the proportion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.160
studios that refuse to work with Netflix has&nbsp;
only increased due to the rise of competitors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.760
By far the biggest competitor against Netflix&nbsp;
is of course Disney+. Disney didn’t even launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.280
their streaming service till November of 2019 or&nbsp;
just over 2 years ago. But, despite their recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.680
entrance into the market, they already have nearly&nbsp;
130 million subscribers. For perspective, Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.920
currently has 221 million subscribers. So, Netflix&nbsp;
does still have about 90 million more subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.720
but if Disney was able to make up half the ground&nbsp;
within 2 years, imagine where they’ll be within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.960
5 or 10 years. It looks to me like it’s just a&nbsp;
matter of time until Disney+ surpasses Netflix in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.240
subscribers, and the key reason for this is that&nbsp;
Disney is a full-on production company. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.840
they have Walt Disney production studios&nbsp;
with all the classics including Aladdin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.680
Alice In Wonderland, Frozen, and Moana. Then,&nbsp;
they have Pixar which includes Soul, Coco, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.280
Incredibles, Cars, Finding Nemo, Inside Out, Toy&nbsp;
Story, and basically every other animated movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.400
you grew up with. And then, they have Marvel which&nbsp;
includes the entire Marvel cinematic universe from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.760
Iron Man to End Game. The only thing they’re&nbsp;
missing is a few Spiderman movies. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.880
they also have the entire Star Wars universe and&nbsp;
we can’t forget about the Mandalorian. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.440
they also have a large library from National&nbsp;
Geographic. And then on top of all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.160
they have a few oddball movies too like Avatar.&nbsp;
And Disney owns the full rights to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.280
everything we covered. With that being said,&nbsp;
Disney isn’t dependent on licensing from anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.400
because they already own everything. In other&nbsp;
words, Disney is a production company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.800
launched a streaming service while Netflix is&nbsp;
a streaming service that does some production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.880
And as more production companies launch their&nbsp;
own streaming services like Paramount plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.840
it’s just going to become harder and harder&nbsp;
for Netflix to retain content and subscribers.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.520
PANDEMIC BLUES:&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.760
Moving onto Netlix’s second-biggest issue, we have&nbsp;
the pandemic. Now, this might be a bit confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.560
as the pandemic was originally hailed as a golden&nbsp;
ticket for stay-at-home businesses like Netflix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.960
But, long term, I think the pandemic was&nbsp;
actually negative for Netflix for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.240
reasons with the first being consumers’ openness&nbsp;
to try other streaming services. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.400
most people who were into streaming shows and&nbsp;
movies already had a Netflix subscription before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.920
the pandemic started. What they didn’t have was&nbsp;
a Disney+ or a Paramount+ or a Hulu subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.080
and the main reason for this was that they didn’t&nbsp;
want to pay for more than 1 streaming service on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.720
a regular basis. When they were stuck at home&nbsp;
though, it made a lot more sense to pick a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.600
more subscriptions. Now, to be honest, most&nbsp;
of these guys have likely gotten spoiled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.640
having all these subscriptions, and they won’t&nbsp;
be canceling any of them anytime soon and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.600
includes me as well. But for people who actually&nbsp;
have a social life and do things other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.720
watching YouTube and movies, it may not make sense&nbsp;
to pay for all these streaming services and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.040
may cut back down to one streaming service. Now,&nbsp;
this brings up the question of which subscriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.160
will they cancel, but honestly, this doesn’t even&nbsp;
matter because the net gain will always be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.520
favor of the competition. For example, Disney+ has&nbsp;
gained 100 million subscribers since the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.800
the pandemic while Netflix has gained 40 million&nbsp;
subscribers. Now, let’s say 10 million subscribers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.760
who have both subscriptions decide to cut down to&nbsp;
just one. Even if all of them choose to stay with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.440
Netflix, Disney+ had a net gain of 90 million&nbsp;
subscribers which is over two times Netflix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.800
Also, realistically, the split will likely be&nbsp;
closer to 50/50 and might even lean in Disney’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.520
favor. Moving onto the second reason that the&nbsp;
pandemic was bad for Netflix in the long run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.480
we have the seriousness of the competition. Before&nbsp;
the pandemic, the streaming business was simply an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.040
addition to many production companies’ businesses.&nbsp;
But during the pandemic, streaming became one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.760
their top priorities. Take Disney for example.&nbsp;
Before the pandemic, they were consistently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.080
launching movies that pulled in over a billion&nbsp;
dollars at the box office, and they had theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.360
parks all around the world. When their revenue&nbsp;
from theatrical releases and theme parks go to $0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.720
overnight, where do you think they’re gonna shift&nbsp;
their focus. The answer is obvious: streaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.440
Many big box films like Soul completely skipped&nbsp;
the theaters and launched directly onto streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.960
services. And it’s not just Disney either,&nbsp;
this scenario is applicable to all production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.600
studios. All of them had much more to gain&nbsp;
from the pandemic, and they all started taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:47.840
streaming much more seriously, so the pandemic was&nbsp;
actually relatively a harmful event for Netflix.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.360
MARKET SATURATION:
Even if the pandemic didn’t happen and Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.240
didn’t have a dependency issue, they still have a&nbsp;
massive hurdle to cross which is sustained growth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.280
Here’s the thing, Netflix has basically put all&nbsp;
of their eggs into the basket video streaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.120
and this makes sense because they need to focus&nbsp;
on maintaining market share against production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:11.040
studios. But, let’s say they do it. Instead of&nbsp;
losing to Disney+, let’s say Disney and Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.240
become a duopoly within the streaming industry,&nbsp;
what happens next? Well, the answer is market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.960
saturation. Sooner or later, everyone who wants&nbsp;
a streaming subscription will have a Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.280
subscription. You could argue that Netflix has&nbsp;
already reached this point given that they missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:30.560
subscriber growth targets and are forecasting weak&nbsp;
growth moving forward themselves. But even if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.360
don’t think Netflix has reached market saturation&nbsp;
quite yet, how much more could they really grow?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:41.200
300, 400, 500 million subscribers? Even if they’re&nbsp;
able to grow to half a billion subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.400
that’s only a little more than a 2x from where&nbsp;
they are today? Now, this isn’t a bad thing, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.840
companies mature, they obviously can’t put up the&nbsp;
same growth numbers. But, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.880
these companies should also no longer be valued as&nbsp;
growth companies. For much of Netflix’s history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.920
their PE ratio has hovered well above 100, and&nbsp;
even with the recent decline, they’ve maintained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.760
a PE ratio over 30. So, maybe the recent decline&nbsp;
is just the market adjusting to the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.640
Netflix is no longer the insane growth company&nbsp;
that it once used to be. Every maturing company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.200
faces this same issue sooner or later. Some&nbsp;
of them learn to diversify and grow through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.360
alternate streams of revenue while other companies&nbsp;
just stagnate for a while at the top before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.920
start declining as more innovative companies take&nbsp;
over their market. Apple, for example, faced this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.080
same issue in the mid-2010s. iPhone sales were&nbsp;
not only stagnating but declining. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.000
Apple tried to combat this by raising prices&nbsp;
on their iPhones to increase profit margins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.680
and that’s kind of what Netflix is doing right&nbsp;
now. But, the thing to note is that even Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.240
realized that there was a limit to how much you&nbsp;
could raise prices, and they would lower their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:52.240
launch price with the iPhone 11. So, Netflix can’t&nbsp;
just keep raising prices to substitute for growth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.200
For Apple, the answer to slowing growth was&nbsp;
focusing on services like Apple Music, Apple Care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:03.120
iCloud, Apple TV, and so on and so forth. In other&nbsp;
words, the key was to offer more value to existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.600
customers as opposed to trying to gain new&nbsp;
customers. Netflix is trying to do this as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.640
with services like Netflix Games. But, the problem&nbsp;
with such endeavors is that they’re usually hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.920
or miss. Sometimes, a new service is exactly&nbsp;
what customers were looking for. Other times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.720
not so much. So, the market doesn’t want to give&nbsp;
Netflix too much credit until it’s proven that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.120
they have a future in something other than just&nbsp;
video streaming which they’ve nearly saturated.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.040
UNCERTAIN FUTURE:&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.480
Looking forward, it’s not like Netflix is&nbsp;
going anywhere anytime soon. We can’t say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.640
that Netflix is too big to fail given that&nbsp;
we’ve seen far larger empires getting wiped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.920
out time and time again. But, given Netflix’s&nbsp;
size, this is definitely not something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.480
would happen overnight. If Netflix were to fail,&nbsp;
it would probably be similar to something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.120
Dish network. It would start with a decade of&nbsp;
stagnation followed by a decade of subscriber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.760
losses to the competition followed by the&nbsp;
decade in which Netflix would actually collapse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.600
So, Netflix has at least two decades to&nbsp;
figure something out here whether that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.000
expanding their services into gaming and live&nbsp;
streaming or whether that’s becoming a full-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.360
production studio themselves. Given how much time&nbsp;
Netflix has, you would think that they’re safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.560
but then again, you could’ve said the same thing&nbsp;
about Dish 10 to 15 years ago. Now, personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:24.000
I think Netflix is a much more innovative company&nbsp;
than Dish. After all, Netflix is more of a tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.560
company than a media company, so I’m sure&nbsp;
they’ll come up with some creative solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.760
especially with Reed Hastings at the helm. So, I&nbsp;
doubt that Netflix will end up like Dish, but only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:37.200
time will tell. Who do you guys think will win&nbsp;
the streaming wars? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.480
drop a like if you think that Netflix will&nbsp;
always be the OG streaming service. And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.640
course, consider checking out our international&nbsp;
channels to watch our videos in other languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.360
and consider subscribing to see&nbsp;
more questions logically answered.

